Hand Gesture Audio Playback System

This project involves creating the necessary electronics to use a proximity sensor (CNY70) that detects when a hand passes a few millimeters above it. When triggered, it plays an audio track stored on a microSD card through an amplifier and speaker. The audio continues to play until either the hand is passed over the sensor again or the audio track finishes.
🛠️ Components Used

    CNY70 Reflective Optical Sensor – Detects the presence of a hand.

    Micro SD Card Module (SPI, 6-Pin, with Level Conversion) – Reads audio files from the microSD card.
    Product Link

    Micro SD Card 4GB – Stores the audio tracks to be played.
    Product Link

    MAX98357 I2S Audio Amplifier Module – Converts digital audio signals to drive the speaker.
    Product Link

    8Ω Speaker – Outputs the audio to the user.
    Product Link

    Arduino UNO R3 / Mega 2560 / Due – Controls the logic, reads the sensor, and handles audio playback.

🔧 How It Works

    The CNY70 sensor detects the presence of a hand by measuring reflected infrared light.

    When the sensor is triggered, the Arduino reads an audio file stored on the microSD card.

    The audio signal is sent via I2S protocol to the MAX98357 amplifier.

    The amplifier drives the 8Ω speaker to output the sound.

    The audio playback continues until:

        The hand is detected again (toggle function), or

        The audio track ends.

  🎶 Usage

    Place audio files (in supported format, e.g., WAV) on the microSD card.

    Power up the system.

    Wave your hand over the sensor to start playback.

    Wave your hand again to stop playback or wait until the track finishes.

📌 Future Improvements

    Add support for multiple audio tracks.

    Implement adjustable volume control.

    Use low-power modes for energy efficiency.
